<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uzun Hasan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1478">1478</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uzun Hasan was the ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> confederation, who died in 1478. He built a powerful state in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Otlukbeli in 1473. Through his daughter he was the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> discuss him in the context of the dynasty's origins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan.jpg" alt="Uzun Hasan" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1467-1473 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uzun_Hasan_on_horse,_1460s%E2%80%931470s,_Aq_Qoyunlu_ruler_Uzun_Hasa_hunting_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Uzun Hasan was born in February or March 1425, the younger son of Ali Beg and Sara Khatun, a daughter of the Bayandur chief Pir 'Ali. His grandfather Qara 'Usman built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> principality through his part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s Anatolian and Syrian campaigns, and Timur recognised him as legitimate ruler of the confederation. When Qara 'Usman died in 1435, a civil war among his sons and grandsons began that lasted more than two decades and drew in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and the rival Qara Qoyunlu. Hasan rose to prominence during it, seized the capital Amid from his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> in a bloodless coup in September 1452, and became undisputed ruler after defeating Jahangir and his Qara Qoyunlu allies in May 1457.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1467 Hasan defeated the Qara Qoyunlu and killed their ruler Jahan Shah, and he later captured and executed the Timurid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-mirza">Abu Sa'id Mirza</a>. These victories brought western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and Arab Iraq under his rule and moved the capital from Amid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. The Venetian Republic, at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, sent a series of ambassadors, among them Lazzaro Querini from 1463 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterino-zeno">Caterino Zeno</a>, who persuaded Hasan to attack the Ottomans. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and Mamluk Syria in 1472 but was decisively defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> at Otlukbeli on 11 August 1473, and no Western power attacked at the same time.</p><p>In his last years his son Ughurlu Muhammad rebelled and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. Hasan died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> in January 1478, and his sons fought each other for the throne. The Venetian ambassador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrogio-contarini">Ambrogio Contarini</a>, who was at his court from 1473 to 1476, described him as tall and lean and about seventy, affable in manner, with trembling hands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Hasan tried to turn a nomadic tribal confederation into a settled Perso-Islamic state. He expanded the central bureaucracy, employed Iranian administrators and issued laws on commerce, civil matters, penalties and taxation, known as the qanun-namas. He had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> translated into Turkic and supported religious bodies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders, including the Safavid order, marrying his sister to its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd">Shaykh Junayd</a> and a daughter to Junayd's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>. Their son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, who founded the Safavid state in 1501. After conquering the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> lands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> preserved Hasan's laws until about 1540.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uzun Hasan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shah Ismail I, founder of the Safavid dynasty that overthrew the Aq Qoyunlu, was <b>Uzun Hasan</b>’s grandson through his daughter by the Trebizond princess Theodora Komnene.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Theodora of Trebizond, daughter of John IV, married the Turkmen ruler <b>Uzun Hasan</b>; their daughter was the mother of Shah Ismail I, founder of the Safavid dynasty of Persia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Hasan Ali, the son who had twice rebelled against him, came to power but was killed by <b>Uzun Hasan</b>, and by 1468 the Aq Qoyunlu under Uzun Hasan (1452–1478), at the height of their power, had defeated the Qara Qoyunlu and conquered Iraq, Azerbaijan and western Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Three things made this possible: the weakness of the Aq Qoyunlu after <b>Uzun Hasan</b>'s death in 1478, the cohesion of the Qizilbash tribal confederacy, and the messianic authority that Ismail's personal charisma gave him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…following decades the Turkmen confederations of the Kara Koyunlu and then the Aq Qoyunlu absorbed the western Persian territories, and Abu Sa'id (r. 1451–1469), the last Timurid to reunite much of the realm, was defeated and killed in 1469 while campaigning against the Aq Qoyunlu leader <b>Uzun Hasan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Erzincan in eastern Anatolia, Ottoman artillery and firearms gave Sultan Mehmed II victory over <b>Uzun Hasan</b> of the Aq Qoyunlu.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.92,40.22&amp;year=1468" aria-label="Diyarbakır on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5553 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Uzun Hasan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6003 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6003 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.92,40.22&amp;year=1468">What was here in 1468? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
